Scott asks to prove the remarks after Definition 7.2 — a nonempty set of natural numbers is recursively enumerable if and only if it is the range of a primitive recursive function, and a computable element, or the graph of a computable map, is enumerated by one primitive recursive function or by a pair of them — and further that every computable element \(y\) equals \(\bigcup_i \uparrow Y_{t(i)}\) for a primitive recursive \(t\) with \(Y_{t(i+1)} \subseteq Y_{t(i)}\). `repred_range_primrec` shows that the range of a primitive recursive function is recursively enumerable: equality of the value on the first component of a code with the second component is decidable, and a number lies in the range exactly when some index forms such a code. `Basic`, packaged as `example_714`, shows the converse for a nonempty set by sending each code to its second component when a decider of a projecting relation accepts and to a known element otherwise, and checks that this primitive recursive function hits exactly the set. `repredu2082` lifts the enumeration to pairs: a nonempty recursively enumerable relation holds of \(n\) and \(m\) exactly when one primitive recursive function returns \(n\) and another returns \(m\), by enumerating the set of paired codes and splitting each value into components. For the chain, `tFun` is the running intersection of an enumerated function against the presentation's intersection; `Basic` proves it is primitive recursive when the enumeration is, by primitive recursion from the value at zero, and proves that a primitive recursive enumeration \(r\) of the index set of a computable element — nonempty because the element contains the master — has running intersection \(t\) a decreasing sequence of neighbourhoods in the element, each contained in the corresponding enumerated neighbourhood because \(t(n+1)\) is the intersection of \(t(n)\) with the next enumerated neighbourhood, and that a neighbourhood lies in the element exactly when it contains some term of the chain, by surjectivity, intersection closure of the element, and upward closure. That is the union of the principal filters. **Theorem:** yes — the range equivalence, the two-function enumeration of a recursively enumerable relation, and the decreasing principal-filter representation are all stated. **Proof:** yes — the range proofs are the code-decider constructions, and the chain proof is enumeration followed by running intersection, with membership by surjectivity, intersection closure, and upward closure.
